@page "/about"

<PageTitle>About Holonet Agents</PageTitle>

<h1>About</h1>

<p class="lead">
    Holonet Agents is a thin client. It hosts no models and stores no data of its own — every answer
    comes from prompt agents that already live in your Microsoft Foundry project. This page describes
    what the app expects from those agents and how it handles their responses.
</p>

<section class="about-section">
    <h2>Agent roster</h2>
    <p>
        The app addresses agents by name. If an agent is missing from the project, the workflow that
        depends on it reports an error instead of silently skipping a step.
    </p>

    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table align-middle">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Agent</th>
                    <th scope="col">Role</th>
                    <th scope="col">Returns</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>holonet-orchestrator</code></td>
                    <td>Classifies an incoming request into a category</td>
                    <td>JSON</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>holonet-character-agent</code></td>
                    <td>Answers character questions</td>
                    <td>JSON</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>holonet-vehicle-agent</code></td>
                    <td>Answers vehicle questions</td>
                    <td>JSON</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>holonet-planet-agent</code></td>
                    <td>Answers planet questions</td>
                    <td>JSON</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>holonet-event-agent</code></td>
                    <td>Answers event questions</td>
                    <td>JSON</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>holonet-jedi-agent</code></td>
                    <td>Answers Jedi questions</td>
                    <td>JSON</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>holonet-sith-agent</code></td>
                    <td>Answers Sith questions</td>
                    <td>JSON</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>holonet-synthesizer-agent</code></td>
                    <td>Rewrites a specialist result into a readable reply</td>
                    <td>Plain text</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="about-section">
    <h2>Response contracts</h2>
    <p>
        Agent output is validated before it reaches the screen. The orchestrator and the specialists must
        return JSON that matches these shapes exactly; unknown properties are rejected so a drifting prompt
        surfaces as a visible error rather than a silently wrong answer.
    </p>

    <div class="contract-grid">
        <div class="contract-card">
            <h3>Orchestrator</h3>
            <pre><code>{ "category": "event" }</code></pre>
            <p>
                One of <code>character</code>, <code>vehicle</code>, <code>planet</code>,
                <code>event</code>, <code>jedi</code>, <code>sith</code>, or <code>other</code>. The
                <code>other</code> category has no specialist, so the request goes straight to the
                synthesizer.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="contract-card">
            <h3>Specialist</h3>
            <pre><code>{ "answer": "…", "success": true }</code></pre>
            <p>
                Both properties are required. Responses wrapped in a Markdown code fence are unwrapped
                automatically, since models commonly add one.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="contract-card">
            <h3>Synthesizer</h3>
            <pre><code>The Battle of Yavin was …</code></pre>
            <p>
                Plain prose, not JSON. Whatever it returns is shown to the reader as the final answer.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="about-note">
        When a response fails validation, the chat shows a contract error along with the raw text the agent
        actually returned, which makes prompt regressions easy to spot.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="about-section">
    <h2>Conversation state</h2>
    <p>
        There is no database. A specialist page keeps a conversation chain with its own agent, so follow-up
        questions retain context. Holonet Chat behaves differently: each submission is a fresh orchestration
        run, because a Foundry response chain belongs to the agent that created it and cannot be shared
        across the orchestrator, a specialist, and the synthesizer. Messages themselves live only in the
        browser circuit and disappear on reload or when you choose <strong>New conversation</strong>.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="about-section">
    <h2>Configuration</h2>
    <p>
        Settings bind from the <code>Foundry</code> configuration section, so they can come from
        <code>appsettings.json</code>, user secrets, or environment variables.
    </p>

    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table align-middle">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Setting</th>
                    <th scope="col">Description</th>
                    <th scope="col">Default</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>Endpoint</code></td>
                    <td>Foundry project endpoint. Required.</td>
                    <td>—</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>DefaultAgentId</code></td>
                    <td>Agent pre-selected on pages that allow a choice.</td>
                    <td>—</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>ManagedIdentityClientId</code></td>
                    <td>User-assigned managed identity for Azure hosting.</td>
                    <td>—</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>AgentListTimeoutSeconds</code></td>
                    <td>Time budget for loading the agent list.</td>
                    <td>30</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>RunTimeoutSeconds</code></td>
                    <td>Time budget for a single agent response.</td>
                    <td>120</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p>
        Authentication uses <code>DefaultAzureCredential</code>. Locally that means <code>az login</code>;
        in Azure it resolves to the app's managed identity. Either identity needs the
        <strong>Azure AI User</strong> role on the project. The agent list is cached once at startup and
        reused, so opening a page does not re-query Foundry.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="about-section">
    <h2>Built with</h2>
    <ul class="about-stack">
        <li>.NET 10 and Blazor Web App with interactive server rendering</li>
        <li><code>Azure.AI.Projects</code> for agent discovery and the responses API</li>
        <li><code>Azure.Identity</code> for credential resolution</li>
        <li>xUnit for the response-contract and workflow-routing tests</li>
    </ul>
</section>
